Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-23
Subject: Keep ncnv set conversions when source and target use different storage kinds
License: expat

`ncnv.pas` had two related bugs in its set-handling type-conversion
machinery:

1. `inserttypeconv` and `inserttypeconv_internal` skipped emitting the
   conversion node whenever `equal_defs` returned true. For sets that's
   wrong: a `smallset` and a normal set can have the same element-base
   type (so `equal_defs` says yes) but completely different storage
   layouts (one fits in a word, the other is a 32-byte bitmask). The
   inline guard tried to special-case this by checking `settype`
   inequality but did so with the wrong polarity for the not-equal-defs
   side. Pull the test out into a `same_set_storage` boolean so both
   conditions read cleanly.

2. The set-loading switch in `typecheckpass` had its smallset/normalset
   logic nested inside the `xor` test, which meant the "same storage"
   path fell out of the conditional without ever consulting the
   `convtype in [tc_equal,tc_not_possible]` check. Restructure the
   nesting so the conversion-or-not decision runs after the
   smallset/normalset decision, not in parallel with it.

Both fixes are semantic -- wrong-storage conversions could previously be
silently dropped at type-check time, and the bootstrap compiler needs
these conversions for correct codegen.

